<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cistercians</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1098–</span> · Also known as Cistercian Order, White Monks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cistercians are a Catholic monastic order founded at Cîteaux in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> in 1098 to follow the Rule of Saint Benedict more strictly. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-clairvaux">Bernard of Clairvaux</a> the order spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, building remote abbeys and developing large farming estates worked by lay brothers. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> the princes of Gwynedd and Deheubarth favoured them, and abbeys such as Aberconwy and Strata <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> kept chronicles of Welsh history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cistercians.jpg" alt="Cistercians" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tardigradius · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_the_Cistercian_Order.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
